Case 3 mirror is committed and pushed (`8484040`) — all four `delete_fixup` cases now have dedicated coverage on both branches. Now for the fuzzer (`tests/fuzz.c`), currently a 12-line stub. This is a different shape of task than the case tests (one cohesive harness rather than a series of small diffs), so here's the full design proposed as one unit before I write anything:

**Design:**
- **Reference model:** a bounded universe of `MAX_KEYS = 500` string keys (`"k0"`..`"k499"`), tracked in a static array `{int present; int value;}` — small enough to keep good insert/overwrite and delete hit/miss ratios over 10^5 ops, no separate heap allocation to manage/leak.
- **Values:** each insert mallocs a fresh `int*`, tree takes ownership via `rb_create(free_int)`. On overwrite, `rb_insert`'s documented contract (frees old value via `value_free`) handles cleanup — no leak. On insert failure (defensive path, contract says caller still owns value on failure), the fuzzer frees the value itself and skips the reference-model update.
- **Ops:** each iteration picks a random key id and one of `{insert, find, delete}` (1/3 each), then checks the operation's result against the reference model:
  - insert → update reference model on success
  - find → assert returned pointer matches (or is NULL iff absent)
  - delete → assert return code (0/-1) matches presence, update reference model
- **Invariant checks:** `rb_size(t)` compared against the reference model's live count after *every* op (cheap O(1) check); `rb_validate(t)` called every 100 ops (per CLAUDE.md's minimum) plus once more at the end.
- **Seed:** defaults to a fixed `12345` for reproducible runs (matches how `make test`/`make asan`/`make memcheck` invoke it with just an op count), overridable via an optional second CLI arg; the chosen seed is printed so any failure can be replayed.
- **Op count:** `argv[1]` (both `make test`/`asan` pass `100000`, `make memcheck` passes `20000`), defaulting to `100000` if omitted.

**Full proposed `tests/fuzz.c`:**
```c
#include <assert.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#include "rbtree.h"

#define MAX_KEYS 500
#define VALIDATE_EVERY 100

typedef struct {
    int present;
    int value;
} ref_entry_t;

static void free_int(void *value) {
    free(value);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    long op_count = (argc >= 2) ? strtol(argv[1], NULL, 10) : 100000;
    unsigned seed = (argc >= 3) ? (unsigned)strtoul(argv[2], NULL, 10) : 12345u;
    printf("fuzz: %ld ops, seed=%u\n", op_count, seed);
    srand(seed);

    static ref_entry_t ref[MAX_KEYS];
    long ref_size = 0;

    rbtree_t *t = rb_create(free_int);
    assert(t != NULL);

    for (long i = 0; i < op_count; i++) {
        int id = rand() % MAX_KEYS;
        char key[16];
        snprintf(key, sizeof(key), "k%d", id);
        int op = rand() % 3;

        if (op == 0) {
            int new_value = rand();
            int *value = malloc(sizeof(*value));
            assert(value != NULL);
            *value = new_value;
            if (rb_insert(t, key, value) != 0) {
                free(value);
            } else {
                if (!ref[id].present) {
                    ref[id].present = 1;
                    ref_size++;
                }
                ref[id].value = new_value;
            }
        } else if (op == 1) {
            void *found = rb_find(t, key);
            if (ref[id].present) {
                assert(found != NULL);
                assert(*(int *)found == ref[id].value);
            } else {
                assert(found == NULL);
            }
        } else {
            int result = rb_delete(t, key);
            if (ref[id].present) {
                assert(result == 0);
                ref[id].present = 0;
                ref_size--;
            } else {
                assert(result == -1);
            }
        }

        assert((long)rb_size(t) == ref_size);
        if (i % VALIDATE_EVERY == 0) {
            assert(rb_validate(t) == 0);
        }
    }

    assert(rb_validate(t) == 0);
    rb_destroy(t);
    printf("fuzz: %ld ops OK\n", op_count);
    return 0;
}
```

Approve this design, or want a different key-universe size / op distribution / seed policy first?

